{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790483937,"nonce":"C6kU1lLnUvHrqn2krlv6VudCYNA=","title":"Python: a zero float remainder takes the dividend's sign, not the divisor's","message":"CPython gives a zero result of float `%` the sign of the divisor: `-0.0 % 7.0` is 0.0, `14.0 % -7.0` is -0.0, `0.0 % -7.0` is -0.0. zypy at cbbca5f6 gets these wrong on both paths:\n\n- typed floats (BinaryOp::FloorRem on f64): `-0.0 % 7.0` prints -0.0 and `14.0 % -7.0` prints 0.0;\n- the dynamic path, zb_mod_f64 in crates/zyntax_builtins/src/dynamic.rs, keeps the dividend sign on purpose (\"as fmod does\"), so boxed `-0.0 % 7.0` prints -0.0 and `0.0 % -7.0` prints 0.0.\n\nReproducer:\n    def f(z: float, d: float):\n        print(z % d, z // d)\n    f(-0.0, 7.0); f(0.0, -7.0); f(-14.0, 7.0); f(14.0, -7.0)\n    xs = [-0.0, 7.0, 0.0, -7.0]\n    print(xs[0] % xs[1], xs[2] % xs[3])\n\nFix: a zero remainder becomes copysign(0.0, divisor) on each tier and in zb_mod_f64.","files":null}]}